WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:20.890
In the early 17th Century, a religious man
decided he was going to uncover the architecture

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:22.650
of the universe itself.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:27.970
Using observations of the Heavens and a deep
understanding of geometry, this man conjectured

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:33.500
a world of harmonious design, where the secrets
of God’s creation could be deciphered by

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.520
anyone willing to use mathematics to look
for them.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.430
In the course of his quest, this man managed
to change our understanding of astronomy forever.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:46.480
His name was Johannes Kepler, and he would
go down as one of the greatest scientists

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:47.899
to ever live.

00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:52.590
Born into a divided Europe, Kepler grew up
during a time of unfathomable change.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:56.890
Witchcraft trials, religious war, and a scientific
revolution were all pulling the continent

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:57.890
apart.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:03.670
Yet Kepler managed to not just navigate through
these changes, but to do so while transforming

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:04.670
science.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:09.200
He discovered laws of astronomy, changed our
understanding of optics, pioneered crystallography,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.200
developed telescopes, and even wrote what’s
likely the first work of science-fiction.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.640
Persecuted for his faith, misunderstood in
his lifetime, this is the life of Johannes

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.320
Kepler, the man who discovered the secrets
of the universe.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:30.960
Children of the Revolutions
When Johannes Kepler was born in 1571, it

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.329
was into a Europe already reeling from two
major revolutions.

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:39.090
Half a century earlier, a man called Martin
Luther had nailed his Ninety Five Theses to

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:46.280
a church door, kicking off a century and a
half of religious upheaval known as The Reformation.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:51.590
Twenty six years after that, in 1543, the
astronomer Nicolaus Copernicus had proved

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:56.430
the sun didn’t orbit the Earth, but the
other way around, triggering an even bigger

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.259
upheaval known as Scientific Revolution.

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:03.840
By the time Johannes Kepler first opened his
eyes, these twin revelations were causing

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.930
Europe to strain at the seams.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:11.590
Not that Kepler’s parents could’ve imagined
he’d live long enough to see those seams

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.040
rip wide open.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.720
The boy was weak, sickly.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.320
A prime candidate in those hard times for
an early death.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.390
And die he nearly did.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:24.410
Before he was even five, Kepler came down
with smallpox, a disease that left his eyesight

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.890
damaged and his hands unable to properly function.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:28.610
Luckily, though, it didn’t kill him.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:33.210
Instead, Kepler lived through his illness
to see the sight that would change his life.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:39.280
The Great Comet of 1577 was so bright it made
the whole of Europe stop and gawp.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.840
Up in the Kingdom of Denmark, the astronomer
Tycho Brahe - who we’re gonna meet again

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:49.370
in a moment - watched the comet pass and knew
it would transform his life’s work.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:52.960
For young Kepler, though, the Great Comet
was even more important.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:56.310
It was this event that would one day make
him into an astronomer.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.670
But only after some false starts.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.730
As a good Lutheran growing up in the Holy
Roman Empire - a collection of several hundred

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:07.780
states roughly analogous to modern Germany
- Kepler wasn’t meant to be a mathematician.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:12.670
When he enrolled at the University of Tübingen
in 1589, it was on the strict understanding

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:17.390
that he would study theology, become a pastor
and help spread Luther’s gospel.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:22.440
The fact this didn’t happen is due entirely
to Michael Maestlin.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:28.280
Maestlin is one of those figures basically
no-one today can name, but who wound up changing

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:29.280
history.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.000
Kepler’s mathematics tutor, Maestlin was
a private devotee of Copernicus - “private”

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.420
because these were the days when saying the
sun didn’t orbit the Earth could end with

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:42.960
the religious authorities using you as an
unwilling test subject for their shiny new

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.050
thumbscrews.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:49.130
But when Maestlin discovered Kepler loved
astronomy, he broke cover long enough to lend

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:53.240
him Copernicus’s De revolutionibus orbium
coelestium libri vi.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.210
What Kepler read there blew his mind.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:04:00.580
In Copernicus’s heliocentric universe, Kepler
saw not heresy, but the first glimpses of

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.310
God’s harmonious design.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:07.790
For Kepler, this was his Road to Damascus
moment, the revelation that changed everything.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:11.650
From then on , he devoted his life to studying
the stars.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:15.650
It was the start of a journey that would take
him not just across the Holy Roman Empire,

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:21.340
but into more danger than this sickly teen
could have ever believed possible.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:30.130
The Road to Prague
In 1594, Johannes Kepler received an unexpected

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:31.130
letter.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:34.060
Now 23, Kepler was on the verge of graduating
university.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:38.040
Despite his devotion to astronomy, he still
planned to become a pastor, because that’s

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.900
just what you did in the late 16th century.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.210
The letter changed all that.

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:47.910
Sent from the Austrian city of Graz, it invited
Kepler to come and work as a math teacher.

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:53.770
And, just like that, Kepler was able to throw
his boring destiny away, and become the mathematician

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.000
he’d always dreamed of being.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:57.680
Well, almost.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.320
Despite what the letter had promised, almost
nobody in Graz attended Kepler’s classes.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.760
He was forced to take a second job as a calendar
maker just to survive.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.630
But what Kepler’s life in Graz lacked in
opportunities, success, or happiness, it more

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:13.150
than made up for with spare time.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:17.100
Spare time in which Kepler could ponder some
of science’s most-pressing mysteries.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:21.710
Before Copernicus had come along and dynamited
thousands of years of human thinking, it was

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:26.810
accepted that the planets were fixed to the
Heavens, and that the Heavens rotated around

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:27.810
the Earth.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:32.810
But now Copernicus had shown Earth was moving
around the sun, all sorts of weird questions

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:33.810
had arisen.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.560
Clearly, Earth wasn’t fixed to anything.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:37.500
It was just kinda floating along.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.190
So what made it float?

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:42.110
What made the other planets move?

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:47.080
It was while daydreaming in one of his near-empty
classes in 1595 that Kepler felt a lightbulb

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.570
go ping! above his head.

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:53.660
If the planets had a regular orbit around
the sun, then surely this was because the

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:57.410
sun itself was affecting them with some kind
of force.

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:02.050
Today, we’d call that force “gravity”,
but Kepler was living about 50 years before

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:06.389
Isaac Newton was even a lustful twinkle in
his father’s eye.

00:06:06.389 --> 00:06:08.410
So he called it magnetism.

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:13.580
Still, it was the first time anyone in Europe
had even come close to understanding what

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:15.780
made our solar system tick.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:20.000
But Kepler went even further than just pontificating
about the sun.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:25.110
Curious about the space between the planets,
he hypothesized that each gap must correspond

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:32.300
to one of the Platonic solids: the tetrahedron,
cube, octahedron, icosahedron, and dodecahedron.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:36.080
When his calculations seemed to bare this
out, he declared he’d uncovered God’s

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:37.080
architecture.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:40.320
So, this might sound a little cuckoo to modern
ears.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.320
Magic shapes?

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:42.370
God’s architecture?

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:43.370
Whaaaa...?

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:45.690
Well, we need to remember the era Kepler was
living in.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:47.840
This was an era before pure mathematics.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:52.750
An era in which you could say the sun orbited
the Earth and not only not have people snigger

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:56.810
at you, but then have them imprison your neighbor
for disagreeing.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:00.630
What Kepler’s work did was suggest the universe
could be understood through math.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:05.520
That there was a scientific way of thinking
about the Heavens beyond simply saying “God

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:06.520
did it.”

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:10.620
It’s that simple thought that would turbocharge
the Scientific Revolution.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:15.470
Kepler published his ideas in 1597, the very
same year he married his first wife.

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:18.820
Not long after, he began writing to Tycho
Brahe.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:19.820
Remember Brahe?

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:23.450
The Danish guy who saw the Great Comet and
realized it would transform his career?

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:28.600
Well Kepler became his pen pal, just as Brahe
was in the process of moving his work to Prague.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.590
It was a relationship that would soon save
Kepler’s life.

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:35.580
In 1599, the religious tensions simmering
across Central Europe between Catholics and

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.230
Lutherans boiled over in Graz.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.610
The city’s Catholics rioted, attacking Protestants.

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:43.730
For the Lutheran Kepler, this was a “hoo,
boy.

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:44.830
Better get outta here,” moment.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:46.870
The trouble was, he had nowhere to go.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:50.780
His religious buddies back at Tübingen University
had read his book and decided he was a Copernicus-loving

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:53.890
heretic who wasn’t allowed to hang out with
them anymore.

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:57.710
Just as it was starting to look like Kepler
might find himself being tarred and feathered

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:00.370
by angry Catholics, Brahe threw him a lifeline.

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:04.970
“Hey, I’ve got a crazy idea,” he basically
said, “why don’t you come work for me

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:05.970
in Prague?”

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:10.250
And that’s how, in the dying days of the
sixteenth century, Johannes Kepler found himself

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:52.240
joining the legendary court of Rudolf II.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:09:13.950
The Court of 
the Golem

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:33.220
If

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:38.650
you were alive and into spooky stuff in 1600,
there was nowhere better to be than Rudolf

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:39.920
II’s Prague.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.880
The Holy Roman Emperor was obsessed with everything
occult.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:47.680
The legendary alchemist John Dee worked for
him, as did Rabbi Loew, the man said to have

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:48.760
created the Golem.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:53.570
Not that Rudolf distinguished between crackpot
mysticism and actual science.

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:58.380
He supported alchemists and astrologers, but
he also astronomers and mathematicians.

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:01.350
And one of those astronomers just happened
to be Tycho Brahe.

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:06.170
Brahe had arrived in Prague the year before,
to take the title Imperial Mathematician.

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:10.100
His mission was to undertake a new set of
planetary observations, and then create a

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:15.380
table from the results that could predict
where anything in the night sky would be on

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:16.410
any future date.

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:20.180
Such tables had existed before, but they’d
been plagued by poor data.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:24.110
Brahe was determined to make the most accurate
table in history.

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:27.510
Unfortunately, history is exactly what Tycho
Brahe was about to become.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:30.580
On October 24, 1601, Brahe died.

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:35.000
Rather than halt his work, Rudolf II simply
handed it to Brahe’s new assistant.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:40.399
Which is how Kepler suddenly found himself
not just saddled with the title Imperial Mathematician

00:10:40.399 --> 00:10:45.380
and a difficult job to finish, but also with
access to the scientific equivalent of magic

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:46.380
pixie dust.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:49.840
In his lifetime, Brahe had been a guy who
didn’t like to share.

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:55.030
Among the things he didn’t like sharing
were his astronomical observations, the most-accurate

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:56.110
in human history.

00:10:56.110 --> 00:11:01.450
So when Kepler inherited Brahe’s work, he
also got his hands on these observations.

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:05.470
This data would be the key that helped unlock
Kepler’s greatest discoveries.

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:09.790
For the next decade, Kepler slaved away in
Prague, making new discoveries like a man

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:10.790
possessed.

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:14.880
In 1604, for example, his attempt to figure
out the best way to observe an eclipse sent

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:20.060
him down a rabbit hole that ended in discovering
how light travels through the human eye.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:23.310
Remember in science class, when you first
found out that the human eye actually projects

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:27.870
stuff onto your retinas upside down and your
brain flips the image the right way up again?

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:30.250
Well, Kepler was the first to discover that.

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:34.000
He then used this work to design highly improved
eyeglasses.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:38.240
The same year he was working on optics, Kepler
also became the first to observe what he called

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:40.120
a “new star” in the sky.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:44.839
Actually, we know now it was the last recorded
supernova in the Milky Way.

00:11:44.839 --> 00:11:49.240
Many years later, Kepler’s observation would
help prove the Earth isn’t the center of

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:50.240
the universe.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:55.149
A few years later, in 1608, he wrote Somnium
- or The Dream in English - possibly the first

00:11:55.149 --> 00:11:56.550
work of science fiction.

00:11:56.550 --> 00:12:01.120
But it was the book Kepler published in 1609
that really changed the world.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:05.250
Astronomia Nova grew out of Kepler’s observations
of Mars’s orbit.

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:09.120
After tracing it across the sky for years,
he finally clicked that the complex, twisting

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:13.740
trail we see from Earth is because Earth is
also moving through space.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:18.529
By deploying what in the 1600s must’ve seemed
like an insane thought experiment, Kepler

00:12:18.529 --> 00:12:22.550
managed to show what Earth’s own orbit would
look like from Mars.

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:27.180
From there, it was just a small step to prove
that Mars’s orbit must be elliptical.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:31.640
It was the first time anyone had figured out
what a planet’s orbit actually looked like.

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:35.910
And it was from here that Kepler drew his
First Law: that the planets have elliptical

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:38.580
orbits, with the sun as one of the foci.

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:42.589
It’s also from here that he drew the Second
of his Three Laws, which is pretty difficult

00:12:42.589 --> 00:12:47.360
to explain, but basically gives you a way
to measure the speed any planet is traveling

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:48.360
around the sun.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:49.399
As for the Third Law…

00:12:49.399 --> 00:12:51.510
Well, we’ll get back to that.

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:56.770
When Kepler published these findings in his
Astronomia Nova in 1609, he impressively included

00:12:56.770 --> 00:13:01.060
all his raw data - today something that’s
a given where science is concerned.

00:13:01.060 --> 00:13:04.029
At the time, though, it was almost like a
dare for his critics.

00:13:04.029 --> 00:13:07.250
A way for Kepler to say: “oh, you think
I’m wrong, do you?

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:10.339
Well you study the data then, and show me
where I’m wrong!”

00:13:10.339 --> 00:13:15.930
The Astronomia Nova was a monumental achievement,
one that cemented Kepler’s claim to greatness.

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:21.910
Unfortunately, 1609 was also the moment Kepler’s
world finally began to split apart.

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:30.010
The Religious Problem
Back in 1555, the Holy Roman Empire had managed

00:13:30.010 --> 00:13:33.870
to stop the continent exploding with something
called the Peace of Augsburg.

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:39.150
After Martin Luther did his thing, the Empire
had transformed into a gigantic pressure cooker,

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:43.260
with Catholics and Lutherans threatening to
unleash Holy War on one another.

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:47.400
The Peace had released some of that pressure
by removing the requirement for states in

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:48.670
the Empire to be Catholic.

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:53.920
Instead, each territory’s ruler could choose
for themselves whether to be Catholic or Lutheran

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:55.580
and no-one else would interfere.

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:59.660
But the Peace was just a temporary fix, a
quick release of some steam.

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:04.769
Inside individual states, pressure was still
rising as the growing popularity of Protestantism

00:14:04.769 --> 00:14:07.170
collided with the often-Catholic elite.

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:12.980
In Bohemia, the Catholic Rudolf II had tried
to solve this by signing the Letter of Majesty.

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:17.550
An attempt at compromise, it allowed Bohemia’s
protestant nobles to build their own churches

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:20.130
and worship how they wished.

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:24.100
Unfortunately, it was this very act of compromise
that would ensure the pressure cooker of Europe

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:26.940
finally exploded into the Thirty Years’
War.

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:29.399
Not that Kepler noticed any of this.

00:14:29.399 --> 00:14:34.230
As the new decade dawned, he was still beavering
away in Prague, still making discoveries.

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:39.440
In 1610, for example, Galileo used the first
telescope to discover Jupiter’s moons Io,

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:43.340
Callisto, Europa, and Ganymede - now know
as the Galilean Moons.

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:48.060
Their existence was such a controversial discovery
that most people simply refused to believe

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:51.630
it, claiming the telescope must be causing
optical illusions.

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:56.330
So Kepler sat down and wrote another book
on optics, which not only proved Galileo couldn’t

00:14:56.330 --> 00:15:00.980
be seeing things, but also described how to
build a vastly more powerful telescope.

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:06.000
If it’s starting to sound like Kepler could
do just about anything he put his mind to,

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:07.490
you haven’t heard the half of it.

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:12.620
The following winter, 1611, he was inspired
to do a short study of snowflakes.

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:16.410
Not only did he become the first person in
history to observe that all snowflakes are

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:20.130
unique, the resulting pamphlet has been called
the earliest work on crystallography.

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:24.140
Anyone else out there feeling like an underachiever
right now?

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:30.130
But even as Kepler kept churning out discovery
after discovery, storm clouds were gathering

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:31.390
on the horizon.

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:35.110
The same year that Kepler was writing about
snowflakes, Rudolf’s younger brother Matthias

00:15:35.110 --> 00:15:38.100
forced the Emperor to abdicate at swordpoint.

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:42.529
From the moment he took the throne, the hardcore
Catholic Matthias made it clear that he wasn’t

00:15:42.529 --> 00:15:46.360
going to honor any letters giving legal rights
to Protestant scum.

00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:50.630
Suddenly, the Lutheran Kepler was living in
a city that officially hated Lutherans.

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:51.880
It was Graz 2.0.

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:54.589
And, once again, Kepler had no way out.

00:15:54.589 --> 00:15:58.910
As 1612 got underway, Kepler began searching
frantically for an exit from Prague.

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:01.980
He wrote again to his old university, but
they were all like “Nah, sorry dude.

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:06.670
We couldn’t possibly hire a guy who thinks
the Earth orbits around the Sun.

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:08.250
People would think we were crazy.”

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:12.529
If that wasn’t bad enough, that same year
Kepler’s wife died of spotted fever.

00:16:12.529 --> 00:16:16.420
Just as Kepler was getting desperate, the
city of Linz came to his rescue.

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:20.490
They would create the position of district
mathematician just for him, provided he used

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:23.709
his time in the city to finish Brahe’s tables.

00:16:23.709 --> 00:16:25.839
With no other options, Kepler accepted.

00:16:25.839 --> 00:16:27.890
He got out just in time.

00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:32.490
Five years later, in 1617, Prague cracked
down hard on its Protestants.

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:38.080
The churches opened under Rudolf II were closed,
non-Catholics subjected to harassment.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:40.730
For a year, the Protestants tried to put up
with these indignities.

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:44.800
Then, in 1618, they finally snapped.

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:49.460
On May 23, a group of Protestants stormed
into Prague castle, grabbed three Catholics

00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:51.620
and hurled them out a high window.

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:55.890
The Defenestration of Prague is famous today
not for its death toll - all three Catholics

00:16:55.890 --> 00:17:01.050
survived after landing in a pile of manure
- but for what it started.

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:05.329
The defenestration was the opening salvo in
the Thirty Years’ War, three decades of

00:17:05.329 --> 00:17:11.899
religious violence that would destroy central
Europe and leave over 8 million dead.

00:17:11.899 --> 00:17:18.369
And Johannes Kepler was about to get caught
right in the middle of it.

00:17:18.369 --> 00:17:23.600
War and Witchcraft
If we were to try and explain the Thirty Years’

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:28.329
War, it would take about, well, thirty years
and leave us all with very sore brains.

00:17:28.329 --> 00:17:32.700
To put it in its crudest terms, just know
that it was a religious war that quickly grew

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:36.830
beyond religion, and would ultimately involve
not just the many states of the Holy Roman

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:41.559
Empire, but also France, Spain, Sweden, the
Netherlands, Poland, the Ottoman Empire, the

00:17:41.559 --> 00:17:45.190
Habsburg Lands, England, Scotland, Russia,
and pretty much just any nation that happened

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:48.760
to be passing by and felt like joining this
Royal Rumble.

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:53.200
Oh, and because the war was so big, so long,
and so vicious, it also created the ideal

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:59.380
conditions for outbreaks of plague, which
in turn created the ideal conditions for witchcraft

00:17:59.380 --> 00:18:04.510
panics, which in turn led to some of the largest
mass-executions for witchcraft in human history.

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:05.510
So, yeah.

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:06.510
The Thirty Years’ War.

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:08.759
Not a fun time to be alive in Central Europe.

00:18:08.759 --> 00:18:12.299
Sadly, Central Europe was exactly where Kepler
was.

00:18:12.299 --> 00:18:18.149
By 1619, Linz - like Graz and Prague before
it - was paralyzed by anti-Protestant riots.

00:18:18.149 --> 00:18:20.390
As a guest of the city, Kepler was allowed
to stay.

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:24.309
He and his new family - he’d married local
lass Susan Reuttinger soon after arriving

00:18:24.309 --> 00:18:28.880
in 1613 and they now had three children - were
officially protected.

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:29.950
But unofficially?

00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:33.419
Unofficially, the family had to deal with
prejudice every single day.

00:18:33.419 --> 00:18:37.830
In such circumstances, it’s almost a wonder
Kepler got any work done.

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:39.809
Yet somehow he managed.

00:18:39.809 --> 00:18:43.359
That same year Kepler published his Third
Law, which states that squaring the length

00:18:43.359 --> 00:18:47.919
of any planet’s orbit - in other words,
the length of its year - and dividing that

00:18:47.919 --> 00:18:52.049
by its distance from the sun cubed will return
a constant number.

00:18:52.049 --> 00:18:54.019
It was a powerful mathematic insight.

00:18:54.019 --> 00:18:57.870
But it was also overshadowed by events in
Kepler’s life.

00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:00.869
Remember the book Somnium Kepler published
back in 1608?

00:19:00.869 --> 00:19:03.450
The one we said might be the first work of
science fiction.

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:08.029
Well, we didn’t mention it at the time,
but Somnium includes a little side story,

00:19:08.029 --> 00:19:11.620
one about a son who is a scientist, and a
mother who is a witch.

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:17.309
In 1620, life began imitating Kepler’s art
in the darkest way.

00:19:17.309 --> 00:19:20.989
That year, Kepler’s own mother was imprisoned
for witchcraft.

00:19:20.989 --> 00:19:25.690
Witchcraft accusations during the Thirty Years’
War weren’t to be taken lightly.

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:30.169
This was the same era as the Wurzburg Witch
Trials, in which hundreds of people were burned

00:19:30.169 --> 00:19:31.169
at the stake.

00:19:31.169 --> 00:19:34.690
Kepler knew he had to save his mother, so
he dropped what he was doing and returned

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:36.919
at once to organize her defense.

00:19:36.919 --> 00:19:39.889
The resulting trial would eat up over a year
of his life.

00:19:39.889 --> 00:19:43.249
To hear the story now is actually all sorts
of impressive.

00:19:43.249 --> 00:19:47.100
Kepler approached the case like he did his
scientific work, using a deep analysis of

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:51.039
the raw documents to forensically examine
the prosecution’s claims.

00:19:51.039 --> 00:19:53.340
It was a battle between science and superstition.

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:59.090
A case in which one side used rational arguments,
while the other talked about demons and women

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:01.690
turning themselves into cats.

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:03.359
Incredibly, science won.

00:20:03.359 --> 00:20:08.139
After 14 months manacled in a cell, the threat
of torture looming over her, Kepler’s mother

00:20:08.139 --> 00:20:11.179
was set free, thanks entirely to her son’s
efforts.

00:20:11.179 --> 00:20:15.309
For Kepler - heck, for rationality - this
was a huge victory.

00:20:15.309 --> 00:20:17.260
But it was one that came at a price.

00:20:17.260 --> 00:20:20.080
For over a year, Kepler’s scientific work
had been put on hold.

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:24.419
The trial had also left him in dire need of
money, money the city of Linz wasn’t willing

00:20:24.419 --> 00:20:25.419
to pay.

00:20:25.419 --> 00:20:29.480
Not long after the trial ended, Kepler’s
Linz home was requisitioned and turned into

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:30.609
a troop garrison.

00:20:30.609 --> 00:20:35.629
In 1625, the Catholics running the city confiscated
his entire library and forced his children

00:20:35.629 --> 00:20:37.009
to start attending Mass.

00:20:37.009 --> 00:20:41.539
Finally, in 1626, Kepler and his family left
Linz for good.

00:20:41.539 --> 00:20:44.799
By now they were broke and on the verge of
destitution.

00:20:44.799 --> 00:20:48.739
Ever the optimist, Kepler tried heading back
to Prague and asking the treasury for unpaid

00:20:48.739 --> 00:20:53.600
wages Rudolf II had owed him, but they simply
laughed him out the city.

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:56.600
It was a bleak time, not just for Kepler,
but for the whole of Europe.

00:20:56.600 --> 00:20:59.009
But the astronomer wasn’t done yet.

00:20:59.009 --> 00:21:02.580
He still had one last moment of greatness
to come.

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:11.309
Into Eternity
It had been 1599 when Rudolf II first called

00:21:11.309 --> 00:21:14.720
Tycho Brahe to his court to prepare new astronomical
tables.

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:17.830
This was years before Kepler described his
Three Laws.

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:22.239
Years before his discovery of Mars’s orbit,
before the Defenestration of Prague, before

00:21:22.239 --> 00:21:23.609
the Thirty Years’ War.

00:21:23.609 --> 00:21:26.480
In that time, Brahe and Rudolf had both died.

00:21:26.480 --> 00:21:27.929
The continent had split apart.

00:21:27.929 --> 00:21:30.460
But Kepler hadn’t forgotten what he was
meant to be doing.

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:33.669
Hadn’t forgotten the promise he made, so
long ago.

00:21:33.669 --> 00:21:37.850
In 1627, Kepler finally published Brahe’s
finished tables.

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:41.729
Known today as the Rudolphine Tables, they
were the most accurate tables for predicting

00:21:41.729 --> 00:21:44.070
the movement of the planets in human history.

00:21:44.070 --> 00:21:49.390
They were based not just on Brahe’s jealously-guarded
data, but also on Kepler’s own observations.

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:54.440
In fact, his Three Laws were baked into the
Tables, underlying every calculation Kepler

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:55.440
had performed.

00:21:55.440 --> 00:21:58.729
If his Laws were real, the predictions in
the Tables would be borne out.

00:21:58.729 --> 00:22:02.679
And, luckily, there was a major one coming
up that everyone could check them against.

00:22:02.679 --> 00:22:06.619
In 1631, Mercury was scheduled to transit
across the sun.

00:22:06.619 --> 00:22:10.389
Sadly for our story, Kepler wouldn’t live
to see himself vindicated.

00:22:10.389 --> 00:22:15.520
On November 15, 1630, Johannes Kepler passed
away in the city of Regensburg.

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:20.649
Around him, the Thirty Years’ War continued
to rage, a pointless howl of destruction consuming

00:22:20.649 --> 00:22:21.649
Europe.

00:22:21.649 --> 00:22:23.450
Even in death, Kepler wouldn’t escape it.

00:22:23.450 --> 00:22:26.919
At some point, a rampaging army destroyed
the cemetery he was buried in.

00:22:26.919 --> 00:22:29.919
The location of his body is now lost to history.

00:22:29.919 --> 00:22:34.299
But even as Europe tore itself apart, the
first rays of hope were beginning to glimmer.

00:22:34.299 --> 00:22:39.750
In 1631, Mercury completed its transit across
the sun, exactly as Kepler’s Tables had

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:40.750
predicted it would.

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:44.070
With Kepler’s work clearly on the mark,
it started raising questions, one of which

00:22:44.070 --> 00:22:48.059
would eventually come to greatly trouble a
young British man.

00:22:48.059 --> 00:22:52.230
About fifty years later, long after the Thirty
Years’ War had finally ended, Isaac Newton

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:54.629
found himself pondering Kepler’s Tables.

00:22:54.629 --> 00:22:59.950
If it were possible to predict the movements
of planets so accurately, there must be some

00:22:59.950 --> 00:23:00.950
force guiding them.

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:03.960
Newton disagreed with Kepler’s theory about
a magnetic sun.

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:08.299
So he set about discovering just what this
mysterious force could be.

00:23:08.299 --> 00:23:11.389
From these musings would come the discovery
of gravity.

00:23:11.389 --> 00:23:16.859
But while his influence on Newton may be Kepler’s
biggest claim to fame, there are other ways

00:23:16.859 --> 00:23:18.139
his work lasted.

00:23:18.139 --> 00:23:22.850
Just take a look at Kepler’s Conjecture,
a 1611 theory on the stacking of cannonballs

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:27.870
that wasn’t proved until 1998, nearly 400
years later.

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:32.820
But perhaps the most important aspect of Kepler’s
work is the thinking that underlay it all.

00:23:32.820 --> 00:23:37.489
Kepler was one of the first to look at the
night sky and see not some unknowable mechanism

00:23:37.489 --> 00:23:43.269
at work, but a divine plan that was both beautiful
and capable of being understood by humans.

00:23:43.269 --> 00:23:48.479
While Kepler may have been more explicitly
religious than most scientists today - he

00:23:48.479 --> 00:23:53.299
actually referred to himself as a prophet
at one point - that idea of a universal language,

00:23:53.299 --> 00:23:57.940
of an existence we can understand through
mathematics, is still relevant.

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:01.840
Kepler may have died before he could see his
life’s work be proved right.

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:11.429
But his name will continue to echo across
history for centuries

00:24:11.429 --> 00:24:31.069
to come.

